.TH "PUT-BUCKET-LOGGING"  "" "" ""
.SH NAME
put-bucket-logging \- 
.SH DESCRIPTION
.sp
\fBWARNING:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
End of support notice: Beginning October 1, 2025, Amazon S3 will discontinue support for creating new Email Grantee Access Control Lists (ACL). Email Grantee ACLs created prior to this date will continue to work and remain accessible through the Amazon Web Services Management Console, Command Line Interface (CLI), SDKs, and REST API. However, you will no longer be able to create new Email Grantee ACLs.
.sp
This change affects the following Amazon Web Services Regions: US East (N. Virginia) Region, US West (N. California) Region, US West (Oregon) Region, Asia Pacific (Singapore) Region, Asia Pacific (Sydney) Region, Asia Pacific (Tokyo) Region, Europe (Ireland) Region, and South America (São Paulo) Region.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNOTE:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This operation is not supported for directory buckets.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Set the logging parameters for a bucket and to specify permissions for who can view and modify the logging parameters. All logs are saved to buckets in the same Amazon Web Services Region as the source bucket. To set the logging status of a bucket, you must be the bucket owner.
.sp
The bucket owner is automatically granted FULL_CONTROL to all logs. You use the \fBGrantee\fP request element to grant access to other people. The \fBPermissions\fP request element specifies the kind of access the grantee has to the logs.
.sp
\fBWARNING:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
If the target bucket for log delivery uses the bucket owner enforced setting for S3 Object Ownership, you can\(aqt use the \fBGrantee\fP request element to grant access to others. Permissions can only be granted using policies. For more information, see \fI\%Permissions for server access log delivery\fP in the \fIAmazon S3 User Guide\fP .
.sp
Grantee Values
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
You can specify the person (grantee) to whom you\(aqre assigning access rights (by using request elements) in the following ways. For examples of how to specify these grantee values in JSON format, see the Amazon Web Services CLI example in \fI\%Enabling Amazon S3 server access logging\fP in the \fIAmazon S3 User Guide\fP .
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
By the person\(aqs ID:  \fB<Grantee xmlns:xsi=\(dqhttp://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema\-instance\(dq xsi:type=\(dqCanonicalUser\(dq><ID><>ID<></ID><DisplayName><>GranteesEmail<></DisplayName> </Grantee>\fP    \fBDisplayName\fP is optional and ignored in the request.
.IP \(bu 2
By Email address:  \fB<Grantee xmlns:xsi=\(dqhttp://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema\-instance\(dq xsi:type=\(dqAmazonCustomerByEmail\(dq><EmailAddress><>Grantees@email.com<></EmailAddress></Grantee>\fP   The grantee is resolved to the \fBCanonicalUser\fP and, in a response to a \fBGETObjectAcl\fP request, appears as the CanonicalUser.
.IP \(bu 2
By URI:  \fB<Grantee xmlns:xsi=\(dqhttp://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema\-instance\(dq xsi:type=\(dqGroup\(dq><URI><>http://acs.amazonaws.com/groups/global/AuthenticatedUsers<></URI></Grantee>\fP
.UNINDENT
.sp
To enable logging, you use \fBLoggingEnabled\fP and its children request elements. To disable logging, you use an empty \fBBucketLoggingStatus\fP request element:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fB<BucketLoggingStatus xmlns=\(dqhttp://doc.s3.amazonaws.com/2006\-03\-01\(dq />\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
For more information about server access logging, see \fI\%Server Access Logging\fP in the \fIAmazon S3 User Guide\fP .
.sp
For more information about creating a bucket, see \fI\%CreateBucket\fP . For more information about returning the logging status of a bucket, see \fI\%GetBucketLogging\fP .
.sp
The following operations are related to \fBPutBucketLogging\fP :
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fI\%PutObject\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fI\%DeleteBucket\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fI\%CreateBucket\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fI\%GetBucketLogging\fP
.UNINDENT
.sp
See also: AWS API Documentation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
  put\-bucket\-logging
\-\-bucket <value>
\-\-bucket\-logging\-status <value>
[\-\-content\-md5 <value>]
[\-\-checksum\-algorithm <value>]
[\-\-expected\-bucket\-owner <value>]
[\-\-cli\-input\-json | \-\-cli\-input\-yaml]
[\-\-generate\-cli\-skeleton <value>]
[\-\-debug]
[\-\-endpoint\-url <value>]
[\-\-no\-verify\-ssl]
[\-\-no\-paginate]
[\-\-output <value>]
[\-\-query <value>]
[\-\-profile <value>]
[\-\-region <value>]
[\-\-version <value>]
[\-\-color <value>]
[\-\-no\-sign\-request]
[\-\-ca\-bundle <value>]
[\-\-cli\-read\-timeout <value>]
[\-\-cli\-connect\-timeout <value>]
[\-\-cli\-binary\-format <value>]
[\-\-no\-cli\-pager]
[\-\-cli\-auto\-prompt]
[\-\-no\-cli\-auto\-prompt]
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH OPTIONS
.sp
\fB\-\-bucket\fP (string) [required]
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The name of the bucket for which to set the logging parameters.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fB\-\-bucket\-logging\-status\fP (structure) [required]
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Container for logging status information.
.sp
LoggingEnabled \-> (structure)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Describes where logs are stored and the prefix that Amazon S3 assigns to all log object keys for a bucket. For more information, see \fI\%PUT Bucket logging\fP in the \fIAmazon S3 API Reference\fP .
.sp
TargetBucket \-> (string) [required]
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Specifies the bucket where you want Amazon S3 to store server access logs. You can have your logs delivered to any bucket that you own, including the same bucket that is being logged. You can also configure multiple buckets to deliver their logs to the same target bucket. In this case, you should choose a different \fBTargetPrefix\fP for each source bucket so that the delivered log files can be distinguished by key.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
TargetGrants \-> (list)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Container for granting information.
.sp
Buckets that use the bucket owner enforced setting for Object Ownership don\(aqt support target grants. For more information, see \fI\%Permissions for server access log delivery\fP in the \fIAmazon S3 User Guide\fP .
.sp
(structure)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Container for granting information.
.sp
Buckets that use the bucket owner enforced setting for Object Ownership don\(aqt support target grants. For more information, see \fI\%Permissions server access log delivery\fP in the \fIAmazon S3 User Guide\fP .
.sp
Grantee \-> (structure)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Container for the person being granted permissions.
.sp
DisplayName \-> (string)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Screen name of the grantee.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
EmailAddress \-> (string)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Email address of the grantee.
.sp
\fBNOTE:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Using email addresses to specify a grantee is only supported in the following Amazon Web Services Regions:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
US East (N. Virginia)
.IP \(bu 2
US West (N. California)
.IP \(bu 2
US West (Oregon)
.IP \(bu 2
Asia Pacific (Singapore)
.IP \(bu 2
Asia Pacific (Sydney)
.IP \(bu 2
Asia Pacific (Tokyo)
.IP \(bu 2
Europe (Ireland)
.IP \(bu 2
South America (São Paulo)
.UNINDENT
.sp
For a list of all the Amazon S3 supported Regions and endpoints, see \fI\%Regions and Endpoints\fP in the Amazon Web Services General Reference.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
ID \-> (string)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The canonical user ID of the grantee.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Type \-> (string) [required]
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Type of grantee
.sp
Possible values:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCanonicalUser\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAmazonCustomerByEmail\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBGroup\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
URI \-> (string)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
URI of the grantee group.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Permission \-> (string)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Logging permissions assigned to the grantee for the bucket.
.sp
Possible values:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBFULL_CONTROL\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBREAD\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBWRITE\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
TargetPrefix \-> (string) [required]
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
A prefix for all log object keys. If you store log files from multiple Amazon S3 buckets in a single bucket, you can use a prefix to distinguish which log files came from which bucket.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
TargetObjectKeyFormat \-> (structure)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Amazon S3 key format for log objects.
.sp
SimplePrefix \-> (structure)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
To use the simple format for S3 keys for log objects. To specify SimplePrefix format, set SimplePrefix to {}.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
PartitionedPrefix \-> (structure)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Partitioned S3 key for log objects.
.sp
PartitionDateSource \-> (string)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Specifies the partition date source for the partitioned prefix. \fBPartitionDateSource\fP can be \fBEventTime\fP or \fBDeliveryTime\fP .
.sp
For \fBDeliveryTime\fP , the time in the log file names corresponds to the delivery time for the log files.
.sp
For \fBEventTime\fP , The logs delivered are for a specific day only. The year, month, and day correspond to the day on which the event occurred, and the hour, minutes and seconds are set to 00 in the key.
.sp
Possible values:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBEventTime\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDeliveryTime\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
JSON Syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
{
  \(dqLoggingEnabled\(dq: {
    \(dqTargetBucket\(dq: \(dqstring\(dq,
    \(dqTargetGrants\(dq: [
      {
        \(dqGrantee\(dq: {
          \(dqDisplayName\(dq: \(dqstring\(dq,
          \(dqEmailAddress\(dq: \(dqstring\(dq,
          \(dqID\(dq: \(dqstring\(dq,
          \(dqType\(dq: \(dqCanonicalUser\(dq|\(dqAmazonCustomerByEmail\(dq|\(dqGroup\(dq,
          \(dqURI\(dq: \(dqstring\(dq
        },
        \(dqPermission\(dq: \(dqFULL_CONTROL\(dq|\(dqREAD\(dq|\(dqWRITE\(dq
      }
      ...
    ],
    \(dqTargetPrefix\(dq: \(dqstring\(dq,
    \(dqTargetObjectKeyFormat\(dq: {
      \(dqSimplePrefix\(dq: {

      },
      \(dqPartitionedPrefix\(dq: {
        \(dqPartitionDateSource\(dq: \(dqEventTime\(dq|\(dqDeliveryTime\(dq
      }
    }
  }
}
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fB\-\-content\-md5\fP (string)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The MD5 hash of the \fBPutBucketLogging\fP request body.
.sp
For requests made using the Amazon Web Services Command Line Interface (CLI) or Amazon Web Services SDKs, this field is calculated automatically.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fB\-\-checksum\-algorithm\fP (string)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Indicates the algorithm used to create the checksum for the request when you use the SDK. This header will not provide any additional functionality if you don\(aqt use the SDK. When you send this header, there must be a corresponding \fBx\-amz\-checksum\fP or \fBx\-amz\-trailer\fP header sent. Otherwise, Amazon S3 fails the request with the HTTP status code \fB400 Bad Request\fP . For more information, see \fI\%Checking object integrity\fP in the \fIAmazon S3 User Guide\fP .
.sp
If you provide an individual checksum, Amazon S3 ignores any provided \fBChecksumAlgorithm\fP parameter.
.sp
Possible values:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCRC32\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCRC32C\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSHA1\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSHA256\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCRC64NVME\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fB\-\-expected\-bucket\-owner\fP (string)
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The account ID of the expected bucket owner. If the account ID that you provide does not match the actual owner of the bucket, the request fails with the HTTP status code \fB403 Forbidden\fP (access denied).
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fB\-\-cli\-input\-json\fP | \fB\-\-cli\-input\-yaml\fP (string)
Reads arguments from the JSON string provided. The JSON string follows the format provided by \fB\-\-generate\-cli\-skeleton\fP\&. If other arguments are provided on the command line, those values will override the JSON\-provided values. It is not possible to pass arbitrary binary values using a JSON\-provided value as the string will be taken literally. This may not be specified along with \fB\-\-cli\-input\-yaml\fP\&.
.sp
\fB\-\-generate\-cli\-skeleton\fP (string)
Prints a JSON skeleton to standard output without sending an API request. If provided with no value or the value \fBinput\fP, prints a sample input JSON that can be used as an argument for \fB\-\-cli\-input\-json\fP\&. Similarly, if provided \fByaml\-input\fP it will print a sample input YAML that can be used with \fB\-\-cli\-input\-yaml\fP\&. If provided with the value \fBoutput\fP, it validates the command inputs and returns a sample output JSON for that command. The generated JSON skeleton is not stable between versions of the AWS CLI and there are no backwards compatibility guarantees in the JSON skeleton generated.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.sp
\fB\-\-debug\fP (boolean)
.sp
Turn on debug logging.
.sp
\fB\-\-endpoint\-url\fP (string)
.sp
Override command\(aqs default URL with the given URL.
.sp
\fB\-\-no\-verify\-ssl\fP (boolean)
.sp
By default, the AWS CLI uses SSL when communicating with AWS services. For each SSL connection, the AWS CLI will verify SSL certificates. This option overrides the default behavior of verifying SSL certificates.
.sp
\fB\-\-no\-paginate\fP (boolean)
.sp
Disable automatic pagination. If automatic pagination is disabled, the AWS CLI will only make one call, for the first page of results.
.sp
\fB\-\-output\fP (string)
.sp
The formatting style for command output.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
json
.IP \(bu 2
text
.IP \(bu 2
table
.IP \(bu 2
yaml
.IP \(bu 2
yaml\-stream
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fB\-\-query\fP (string)
.sp
A JMESPath query to use in filtering the response data.
.sp
\fB\-\-profile\fP (string)
.sp
Use a specific profile from your credential file.
.sp
\fB\-\-region\fP (string)
.sp
The region to use. Overrides config/env settings.
.sp
\fB\-\-version\fP (string)
.sp
Display the version of this tool.
.sp
\fB\-\-color\fP (string)
.sp
Turn on/off color output.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
on
.IP \(bu 2
off
.IP \(bu 2
auto
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fB\-\-no\-sign\-request\fP (boolean)
.sp
Do not sign requests. Credentials will not be loaded if this argument is provided.
.sp
\fB\-\-ca\-bundle\fP (string)
.sp
The CA certificate bundle to use when verifying SSL certificates. Overrides config/env settings.
.sp
\fB\-\-cli\-read\-timeout\fP (int)
.sp
The maximum socket read time in seconds. If the value is set to 0, the socket read will be blocking and not timeout. The default value is 60 seconds.
.sp
\fB\-\-cli\-connect\-timeout\fP (int)
.sp
The maximum socket connect time in seconds. If the value is set to 0, the socket connect will be blocking and not timeout. The default value is 60 seconds.
.sp
\fB\-\-cli\-binary\-format\fP (string)
.sp
The formatting style to be used for binary blobs. The default format is base64. The base64 format expects binary blobs to be provided as a base64 encoded string. The raw\-in\-base64\-out format preserves compatibility with AWS CLI V1 behavior and binary values must be passed literally. When providing contents from a file that map to a binary blob \fBfileb://\fP will always be treated as binary and use the file contents directly regardless of the \fBcli\-binary\-format\fP setting. When using \fBfile://\fP the file contents will need to properly formatted for the configured \fBcli\-binary\-format\fP\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
base64
.IP \(bu 2
raw\-in\-base64\-out
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fB\-\-no\-cli\-pager\fP (boolean)
.sp
Disable cli pager for output.
.sp
\fB\-\-cli\-auto\-prompt\fP (boolean)
.sp
Automatically prompt for CLI input parameters.
.sp
\fB\-\-no\-cli\-auto\-prompt\fP (boolean)
.sp
Disable automatically prompt for CLI input parameters.
.SH EXAMPLES
.sp
\fBNOTE:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
To use the following examples, you must have the AWS CLI installed and configured. See the \fI\%Getting started guide\fP in the \fIAWS CLI User Guide\fP for more information.
.sp
Unless otherwise stated, all examples have unix\-like quotation rules. These examples will need to be adapted to your terminal\(aqs quoting rules. See \fI\%Using quotation marks with strings\fP in the \fIAWS CLI User Guide\fP .
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBExample 1: To set bucket policy logging\fP
.sp
The following \fBput\-bucket\-logging\fP example sets the logging policy for \fIamzn\-s3\-demo\-bucket\fP\&. First, grant the logging service principal permission in your bucket policy using the \fBput\-bucket\-policy\fP command.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
aws s3api put\-bucket\-policy \e
    \-\-bucket amzn\-s3\-demo\-bucket \e
    \-\-policy file://policy.json
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Contents of \fBpolicy.json\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
{
    \(dqVersion\(dq: \(dq2012\-10\-17\(dq,
    \(dqStatement\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqSid\(dq: \(dqS3ServerAccessLogsPolicy\(dq,
            \(dqEffect\(dq: \(dqAllow\(dq,
            \(dqPrincipal\(dq: {\(dqService\(dq: \(dqlogging.s3.amazonaws.com\(dq},
            \(dqAction\(dq: \(dqs3:PutObject\(dq,
            \(dqResource\(dq: \(dqarn:aws:s3:::amzn\-s3\-demo\-bucket/Logs/*\(dq,
            \(dqCondition\(dq: {
                \(dqArnLike\(dq: {\(dqaws:SourceARN\(dq: \(dqarn:aws:s3:::SOURCE\-BUCKET\-NAME\(dq},
                \(dqStringEquals\(dq: {\(dqaws:SourceAccount\(dq: \(dqSOURCE\-AWS\-ACCOUNT\-ID\(dq}
            }
        }
    ]
}
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
To apply the logging policy, use \fBput\-bucket\-logging\fP\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
aws s3api put\-bucket\-logging \e
    \-\-bucket amzn\-s3\-demo\-bucket \e
    \-\-bucket\-logging\-status file://logging.json
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Contents of \fBlogging.json\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
{
     \(dqLoggingEnabled\(dq: {
         \(dqTargetBucket\(dq: \(dqamzn\-s3\-demo\-bucket\(dq,
         \(dqTargetPrefix\(dq: \(dqLogs/\(dq
     }
 }
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNOTE:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The \fBput\-bucket\-policy\fP command is required to grant \fBs3:PutObject\fP permissions to the logging service principal.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
For more information, see \fI\%Amazon S3 Server Access Logging\fP in the \fIAmazon S3 User Guide\fP\&.
.sp
\fBExample 2: To set a bucket policy for logging access to only a single user\fP
.sp
The following \fBput\-bucket\-logging\fP example sets the logging policy for \fIamzn\-s3\-demo\-bucket\fP\&. The AWS user \fIbob@example.com\fP will have full control over
the log files, and no one else has any access. First, grant S3 permission with \fBput\-bucket\-acl\fP\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
aws s3api put\-bucket\-acl \e
    \-\-bucket amzn\-s3\-demo\-bucket \e
    \-\-grant\-write URI=http://acs.amazonaws.com/groups/s3/LogDelivery \e
    \-\-grant\-read\-acp URI=http://acs.amazonaws.com/groups/s3/LogDelivery
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Then apply the logging policy using \fBput\-bucket\-logging\fP\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
aws s3api put\-bucket\-logging \e
    \-\-bucket amzn\-s3\-demo\-bucket \e
    \-\-bucket\-logging\-status file://logging.json
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Contents of \fBlogging.json\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.nf
.ft C
{
    \(dqLoggingEnabled\(dq: {
        \(dqTargetBucket\(dq: \(dqamzn\-s3\-demo\-bucket\(dq,
        \(dqTargetPrefix\(dq: \(dqamzn\-s3\-demo\-bucket\-logs/\(dq,
        \(dqTargetGrants\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqGrantee\(dq: {
                    \(dqType\(dq: \(dqAmazonCustomerByEmail\(dq,
                    \(dqEmailAddress\(dq: \(dqbob@example.com\(dq
                },
                \(dqPermission\(dq: \(dqFULL_CONTROL\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNOTE:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
the \fBput\-bucket\-acl\fP command is required to grant S3\(aqs log delivery system the necessary permissions (write and read\-acp permissions).
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
For more information, see \fI\%Amazon S3 Server Access Logging\fP in the \fIAmazon S3 Developer Guide\fP\&.
.SH OUTPUT
.sp
None
